# Prod-environment Caddyfile (docs/plans/prod-deploy.md §3 Session 2). Copied from
# deploy/Caddyfile (dev) as the stated starting point — differences from that file:
#   - No `X-Robots-Tag: noindex` anywhere (every host here is a real, indexable prod host; dev's
#     own file flags this exact line as the one to drop when prod gets its own Caddyfile).
#   - `www.ammari.id` gets its OWN site block that only 301-redirects to the apex — dev instead
#     serves `ammari.my.id`/`www.ammari.my.id` identically from one block. Prod's apex is
#     canonical (docs/plans/prod-deploy.md §2 "www handling" row) so `BETTER_AUTH_URL`/
#     `metadataBase` resolve consistently.
#   - `client_auth`'s `trust_pool` reads prod's OWN zone-level AOP CA
#     (/etc/caddy/cloudflare/origin-pull-ca.pem — a CA we generated ourselves, see
#     cloudflare/README.md), not Cloudflare's published Global AOP CA that dev's identically-named
#     path holds. Different bytes, same filename/mount point, by design (deploy-prod/ is a fully
#     separate directory from deploy/ — docs/plans/prod-deploy.md §2 "Config location" row).
#   - `reverse_proxy` upstreams are NOT a literal container name here — they're an `import` of a
#     generated snippet (deploy-prod/caddy/active-upstream.caddy) that names whichever blue/green
#     slot is currently active. Session 3's deploy script rewrites that file and runs
#     `caddy reload` on every slot flip; this Caddyfile itself never changes for a routine deploy.
#
# The Cloudflare trust boundary lives entirely here, not in ufw (docker publishes its own
# iptables rules that bypass ufw's filtering of published ports — see docs/plans/deploy-dev.md):
#   1. PRIMARY: Cloudflare Authenticated Origin Pulls — `client_auth` below requires a client
#      certificate, signed by OUR OWN CA, on every TLS handshake. A request that didn't actually
#      come through Cloudflare (direct to the VPS IP) is rejected before any application code —
#      or even an HTTP response — runs. This is ZONE-LEVEL AOP (cloudflare/README.md), not the
#      Global toggle dev uses: Cloudflare presents a leaf cert we generated and uploaded
#      ourselves, and only our own CA root (never the leaf's private key) lives on this VPS.
#   2. DEFENSE IN DEPTH: `cloudflare_only` (imported from cloudflare/cloudflare-only.caddy,
#      generated by deploy/update-cloudflare-ips.sh from the shared cloudflare-ips.txt) rejects
#      anything whose remote IP isn't in Cloudflare's published ranges.
#   3. `trusted_proxies` (same source) makes access logs — and `cf-connecting-ip`-based rate
#      limiting in packages/auth / apps/web/src/lib/claim/ip.ts — trust the real client IP only
#      when it actually came from Cloudflare's edge.
#
# VERIFY BEFORE RELYING ON THIS: `caddy validate --config /opt/ammari-prod/Caddyfile` against the
# pinned caddy:2.10.2 image, then the runbook's own "curl the VPS IP directly, bypassing
# Cloudflare, must fail" step (docs/deploy-prod.md, once written in Session 7).
{
	import cloudflare/trusted-proxies.caddy
}

import cloudflare/cloudflare-only.caddy

# Session 3's generated snippet — defines (active_upstream_web) and (active_upstream_admin),
# each a `reverse_proxy` to whichever slot is currently active. The file checked into this repo
# is a sensible DEFAULT pointing at the blue slots (see deploy-prod/caddy/active-upstream.caddy's
# own header comment) so this Caddyfile validates and runs correctly even before Session 3's
# deploy script exists; Session 3 is what actually rewrites it on every slot flip.
import generated/active-upstream.caddy

ammari.id {
	import cloudflare_only
	tls /etc/caddy/certs/origin.pem /etc/caddy/certs/origin.key {
		client_auth {
			mode require_and_verify
			trust_pool file {
				pem_file /etc/caddy/cloudflare/origin-pull-ca.pem
			}
		}
	}
	# /api/revalidate is called ONLY over the INTERNAL Docker network (apps/admin ->
	# http://web:3000, bypassing Caddy entirely — see deploy-prod/docker-compose.yml) and never
	# needs the public domain. Blocked here too, defense in depth: Authenticated Origin Pulls
	# above only proves a request came THROUGH Cloudflare, not that it's a legitimate admin
	# request — every ordinary public visitor is proxied through Cloudflare the same way, so
	# without this block a leaked REVALIDATE_SECRET would still work from the public internet.
	# See docs/plans/storefront-cms.md session 6.
	@revalidate path /api/revalidate*
	respond @revalidate 404
	# /api/test/* (apps/web's e2e preview-token backdoor) must NEVER be reachable from the public
	# internet, full stop — it is gated in code (NODE_ENV != production AND
	# E2E_TEST_LOGIN=true, see src/lib/preview/e2e-gate.ts), but that gate lives in an env var
	# that this Caddy config has no visibility into, so this block is NOT conditional on whether
	# the gate currently happens to be closed. It is the one layer that holds even if
	# E2E_TEST_LOGIN were ever mistakenly set in the real web.env on this server.
	@apitest path /api/test/*
	respond @apitest 404
	import active_upstream_web
}

# Apex is canonical (docs/plans/prod-deploy.md §2 "www handling" row) — this host only redirects,
# it never reverse-proxies. Still behind the SAME Cloudflare trust boundary as every other host
# in this file: a direct, Cloudflare-bypassing request to this hostname must fail exactly like
# ammari.id's and admin.ammari.id's do, not quietly serve a redirect to anyone who can reach the
# VPS IP directly. The TLS setup is identical because it's the same zone/cert/CA, not a special
# case — no separate certificate or AOP configuration is needed for this one extra host.
www.ammari.id {
	import cloudflare_only
	tls /etc/caddy/certs/origin.pem /etc/caddy/certs/origin.key {
		client_auth {
			mode require_and_verify
			trust_pool file {
				pem_file /etc/caddy/cloudflare/origin-pull-ca.pem
			}
		}
	}
	redir https://ammari.id{uri} 301
}

admin.ammari.id {
	import cloudflare_only
	tls /etc/caddy/certs/origin.pem /etc/caddy/certs/origin.key {
		client_auth {
			mode require_and_verify
			trust_pool file {
				pem_file /etc/caddy/cloudflare/origin-pull-ca.pem
			}
		}
	}
	# Same reasoning as web's own /api/test/* block above — apps/admin's e2e sign-in backdoor
	# (/api/test/login) must never be reachable from the public internet regardless of what its
	# own code-level gate currently evaluates to.
	@apitest path /api/test/*
	respond @apitest 404
	import active_upstream_admin
}
